1. create ci workflow
2. migrate cd workflow into single ansible-playbook that get triggered from semaphore webhook/integration 

A production-ready network change can follow this workflow:
Engineer creates code / playbook
↓
Git Repository stores the change
↓
Pull Request enables review and collaboration
↓
CI Pipeline performs automated checks:
✅ Syntax & lint validation
✅ Automated testing
✅ Lab / pre-production deployment
✅ pyATS validation
✅ Security scanning
↓
Approval gate
↓
Automated deployment to production
↓
Post-change verification and reporting"

ci content in .gitea/workflows/ci.yml:
---
name: NetDevOps CI Pipeline

on:
  pull_request:
    branches:
      - main

# Set the default shell to standard sh for Alpine/PyATS container compatibility
defaults:
  run:
    shell: sh

jobs:
  ci-validation:
    name: Lint & Dry-Run Checks
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    container:
      image: cisco-pyats/pyats:latest

    steps:
      - name: Checkout Repository Code
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Install CI Tools (yamllint & ansible)
        run: |
          pip install --no-cache-dir yamllint ansible

      - name: 1. YAML Syntax & Format Linting
        run: |
          echo "=== Running YAML Lint ==="
          yamllint *.yml tasks/*

      - name: 2. PyATS Testbed YAML Validation
        run: |
          echo "=== Validating PyATS Testbed Syntax ==="
          # Validates that testbed.yaml is structured properly according to PyATS schemas
          pyats validate testbed testbed.yaml

      - name: 3. Ansible Playbook Dry-Run (--check)
        run: |
          echo "=== Running Ansible Syntax Check & Dry Run ==="
          # Check syntax first
          ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini playbooks/apply_ospf.yml --syntax-check
          
          # Perform dry-run simulation (no changes applied to live network)
          ansible-playbook -i inventory.ini playbooks/apply_ospf.yml --check --diff
